 "licence_note": null,
 "history": "Mitasses of coarse wool were what Canadians, the colonial regulars on campaign and rangers wore on the legs in the woods: about three quarters of a yard long, cut loose, the seam some four fingers in from the edges so the flap stands outside the leg, tied under the knee (d'Aleyrac; Knox, 1758). Blue, red or green [S2][S1][M1][M3]. Plain new builds of the documented French-Canadian and colonial-regular campaign wear, not the Eastern Woodlands set (the owner's consultation decision, 2026-10-03).",
